/-!
# Integrality over K[j] passes to L[j] in a tower

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.isIntegral_adjoin_of_isScalarTower`
(`888dcf54-bd3c-52ac-9c4a-de111f6a37be`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `8df45cc6-bd84-55d2-8e35-86b947fe5012`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$, $L$ and $F$ be commutative rings equipped with algebra structures making $F$ an algebra
over $L$, $L$ an algebra over $K$ and $F$ an algebra over $K$, with the three structure maps
compatible (an `IsScalarTower K L F` hypothesis: scalar multiplication by $K$ on $F$ factors through
$L$). Let $j$ and $x$ be elements of $F$. The hypothesis is that $x$ is integral over the
$K$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ K\ \{j\}$ of $F$ generated by $j$, that is, $x$ is a root
of a monic polynomial with coefficients in that subalgebra. The conclusion is that $x$ is likewise
integral over the $L$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ L\ \{j\}$ of $F$ generated by $j$. No
finiteness, reducedness or domain assumptions are imposed on any of the three rings.

This is the elementary transitivity observation that enlarging the ring of scalars from $K[j]$ to
$L[j]$ inside $F$ preserves integrality; in the modular-curve part of the development it is used to
pass from integrality over $\mathbb{Q}[j]$ to integrality over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}[j]$, and it is
invoked in the study of $q$-expansions, of finite-dimensionality of algebras generated by $j(q)$,
and of cuspidal classes.

